# PROBABILITY, as a MARKOV CATEGORY.
#
# The thesis (Fritz, *A synthetic approach to Markov kernels,
# conditional independence and theorems on sufficient statistics*;
# READING): a Markov category is a monoidal category in which every
# object carries a comonoid — every wire can be COPIED and DISCARDED —
# and in which copying is NOT NATURAL.  That last clause is the whole
# of probability.  Copy after a coin flip gives two EQUAL bits; two coin
# flips give two INDEPENDENT bits; and a category where those two
# programs agree is a category with no randomness in it.
#
# Braid's base is cartesian.  `dup` there IS natural — `examples/laws.braid`
# has the normalizer PROVE `dup ; f f = f ; dup` for an arbitrary word,
# which is exactly the statement that the base has no randomness.  A
# model of the Doctrine whose hom-object is a STOCHASTIC MAP is a Markov
# category, and `with Enum` is the functor from the deterministic world
# into it.  The distinction is then drawn STRUCTURALLY, not by a
# convention about what `flip` means:
#
#     def twoEqual = use Enum ; half ; flip ; dup        # ONE flip, copied
#     def twoIndep = use Enum ; twoBiases ; bothFlip     # TWO flips
#
# and §6 prints the two distributions, which differ.  Nothing in the
# file declares that they should.  `dup` is transported by the functor
# and the functor is the model; the disagreement is what the model IS.
#
# THREE MODELS of one theory: `Enum` enumerates exactly (the finite
# distribution monad's Kleisli category), `Sampler` threads a random
# number RESOURCE and draws (a small LCG in Braid, so every number
# below is reproducible), `Nondet` keeps only the SUPPORT — the
# possibilistic Markov category, where a weight is a bit.  The
# Doctrine's seven arrow laws run for all three before anything prints.
#
# There is no `Code`, no `functor` and no `getCode` anywhere below.
# Binders and destructuring throughout.
#
# The DataFrame library is this file's client: `import` below brings
# `groupBy`, `sumF`, `keep` and `printFrame` in, and every distribution
# printed is a frame — outcome, weight — with the weights summed per
# outcome by `groupBy`.  A posterior is a frame operation.

import "frame.braid"
#   `Pair` comes from there too: the pairing the Doctrine's strength
#   packs a wide stage with is the same `Pair(a, b) = a b` the frame's
#   `first` unzips.  One spelling per thing, across two files.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The theory: a Markov category, presented
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `in Doctrine` is the declaration that `compose`, `embed` and `first`
# are the prelude doctrine's, at its signatures — so `with M` of a model
# transports base programs into the category M presents, and the
# doctrine's seven arrow laws are inherited.
#
# The GENERATORS are the distributions, and each is an ENTRY: a slot
# that builds a carrier out of nothing, `• ⇒ k(_, _)`, read off its
# declared arrow.  That is the answer to "how does `flip` take its
# Float?" — it does NOT take one.  A hom-object `k(a, b)` names one
# object on each side and there is no `k(•, Bool)`, because `•` is not
# a wire; so a distribution is written as the KERNEL it is, `flip :
# k(Float, Bool)`, the bias arriving on the wire the kernel consumes.
# The bias is then an ordinary base stage INSIDE the transported scope
# — `half = (n -> 0.5)` is one wire in, one wire out, so it embeds —
# and the composition of the scope routes it into `flip`'s domain.
# Nothing special-cases a parameter.
#
# `report` is the EXIT, and its result is a THEORY PARAMETER: what a
# model hands back about a kernel is not one type — `Enum` gives a
# weighted list, `Sampler` a list of draws, `Nondet` a support — so
# `d(_)` is a CONSTRUCTOR parameter the model fills, the same rule
# `examples/autodiff.braid` uses for `gradient : a ⇒ g` one kind up
# (2026-09-15, MANUAL §8).  It is declared at `k(Int, b)` because a
# kernel must be run SOMEWHERE: `Int` is the stand-in for the monoidal
# unit the Doctrine has no name for, and `report` runs at 0.  Every
# program below therefore begins by ignoring an Int.
#
# `condition` is the sub-Markov generator: it takes a Bool beside a
# value and keeps the branch, at weight 1, or kills it.  A category
# with it is AFFINE rather than Markov — §9 prints the mass that says
# so — and Bayes' rule is `condition` plus renormalizing, which is a
# frame operation.
#
# Every slot is `=Recursive>`: `Sampler`'s `report` folds a draw over a
# range and `Enum`'s `compose` walks a list, and a slot declares the
# MOST a model may do.

theory Prob(k(_, _), d(_)) in Doctrine =
    compose   : k(a, b) k(b, c) =Recursive> k(a, c)
    embed     : Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ =Recursive> k(a, b)
    first     : k(a, b) =Recursive> k(Pair(a, c), Pair(b, c))
    observe   : k(Int, Int) =Recursive> Int
    sample    : • =Recursive> k(Int, Int)
    flip      : • =Recursive> k(Float, Bool)
    uniform   : • =Recursive> k(Int, Int)
    condition : • =Recursive> k(Pair(Bool, a), a)
    report    : k(Int, b) =Recursive> d(b)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Model one: `Enum` — exact enumeration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hom-object is a function into a WEIGHTED LIST: the Kleisli
# category of the finite distribution monad.  `compose` is bind with
# the weights multiplied, `first` is the strength, `embed` is the point
# mass.  That is the entire model, and every law it obeys it obeys
# because those three lines are what they are.

data Weighted(b) = List(Box(b Float))
data Kern(a, b)  = Fn⟨a =Recursive> Weighted(b)⟩

## run a kernel at a point, down to the bare list of (outcome, weight)
def atK   = (c x -> c ; unKern ; _ x ; ev ; unWeighted)
## the point mass — `embed`'s whole content, and `dirac` is its name
def dirac = (y -> (y 1.0 ; Box) ; single ; Weighted)

def eEmbed = (f -> [(x -> f ; _ x ; ev ; dirac)] ; Kern)
def scaleBy = (w e -> e ; unBox ; (y v -> y (w v ; fmul) ; Box))
def eCompose =
    c e ->
    [(x -> (c x ; atK)
         ; [(b -> b ; unBox
                    ; (y w -> (e y ; atK) ; [(e2 -> w e2 ; scaleBy)] ... ; map))] ... ; map
         ; concat ; Weighted)] ; Kern
def eFirst =
    c ->
    [(p -> p ; unPair
             ; (x r -> (c x ; atK)
                     ; [(b -> b ; unBox ; (y w -> (y r ; Pair) w ; Box))] ... ; map
                     ; Weighted))] ; Kern

def eFlip = [(p -> ((true p ; Box) (false (1.0 p ; fsub) ; Box) ; pack) ; Weighted)] ; Kern
def eUniform =
    [(n -> (n ; range) ; [(i -> i (1.0 (n ; toFloat) ; fdiv) ; Box)] ... ; map ; Weighted)] ; Kern
def eCondition =
    [(p -> p ; unPair ; (b y -> b (y ; dirac) (nil ; Weighted) ; select))] ; Kern
def eReport = (c -> c ; unKern ; _ 0 ; ev)

## The doctrine's own evidence, and it is STOCHASTIC on purpose: the
## seven arrow laws are then a real test of the model's bind, not of a
## deterministic special case.  The weights are dyadic, so every sum
## and product the laws take is an exact double.
def eSample =
    [(x -> (((x ; _ 1 ; +) 0.5 ; Box) ((x 2 ; *) 0.5 ; Box) ; pack) ; Weighted)] ; Kern

## the expected value of a weighted list of Ints, and the doctrine's
## `observe` built from it — two points, scaled and floored, because
## the doctrine asks an exit for an `Int`
def eExpect =
    w ->
    w ; unWeighted
      ; [(acc b -> b ; unBox ; (y p -> acc ((y ; toFloat) p ; fmul) ; fadd))] 0.0 ... ; fold
def eObserve =
    c ->
    (((c 3 ; atK ; Weighted ; eExpect) (c 4 ; atK ; Weighted ; eExpect) ; fadd) 1000.0 ; fmul)
      ; floor

model Enum in Prob(Kern, Weighted) =
    compose   = eCompose
    embed     = eEmbed
    first     = eFirst
    observe   = eObserve
    sample    = eSample
    flip      = eFlip
    uniform   = eUniform
    condition = eCondition
    report    = eReport

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Model two: `Sampler` — a random-number RESOURCE, threaded
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hom-object is a function that threads an `Rng` — a seed — so a
# kernel is `Rng a ⇒ Rng b` and composition is the ordinary threading
# `examples/metered.braid` does for Fuel.  The generator is a linear
# congruential one, written in Braid over Int, so it is DETERMINISTIC:
# every count printed below is reproducible, and a different seed is a
# different test rather than a different program.

resource Rng = Int
data Draws(b)   = List(b)
data Samp(a, b) = Fn⟨Rng a =Recursive> Rng b⟩

## A 48-bit linear congruential generator — the whole source of
## randomness in this file, four atoms wide.  An Int in Braid is exact
## at any width, so the modulus is written as the number it is.
def stepRng = unRng ; (s -> ((s 25214903917 ; *) 11 ; +) 281474976710656 ; mod)
def drawRaw = (r -> r ; stepRng ; dup ; Rng _)          # Rng ⇒ Rng Int
## …read as a Float in [0, 1), from the TOP 32 bits.  Which bits are
## taken is not a detail: an LCG's low bits have short periods (bit 0
## alternates), so `u mod 2` is the worst coin there is and `u div
## 2^16` is a good one.
def unitOf  = (u -> ((u 65536 ; div) ; toFloat) 4294967296.0 ; fdiv)

def runS    = (c r x -> c ; unSamp ; _ r x ; ev)
def sEmbed  = (f -> [(r x -> r (f ; _ x ; ev))] ; Samp)
def sCompose = (c e -> [(r x -> c r x ; runS ; (r2 y -> e r2 y ; runS))] ; Samp)
def sFirst =
    c ->
    [(r p -> p ; unPair ; (x d -> c r x ; runS ; (r2 y -> r2 (y d ; Pair))))] ; Samp

## a p-coin: one draw in [0, 1), true when it lands below p
def sFlip =
    [(r p -> r ; drawRaw ; (r2 u -> r2 ((u ; unitOf) p ; flt? ; verdict)))] ; Samp
def sUniform =
    [(r n -> r ; drawRaw ; (r2 u -> r2 (((u ; unitOf) (n ; toFloat) ; fmul) ; floor)))] ; Samp
## a sampler cannot drop a branch, so `condition` KEEPS: the model is a
## forward sampler and rejection is not part of it (§12).
def sCondition = [(r p -> p ; unPair ; (b y -> r y))] ; Samp
def sSample =
    [(r x -> r ; drawRaw
           ; (r2 u -> r2 (((u ; unitOf) 0.5 ; flt? ; verdict) (x ; _ 1 ; +) (x 2 ; *) ; select)))] ; Samp

def theSeed  = 20260915
def sOnce    = (c x -> c (theSeed ; Rng) x ; runS ; (r y -> y))
def sObserve = (c -> (c 3 ; sOnce) (c 4 ; sOnce) ; +)

## `report` draws 200 samples, threading ONE seed through all of them —
## which is what makes the tally below a tally and not 200 copies of
## one number.
data Acc(b) = Int List(b)
def drawsWanted = 200
def sInto =
    c ->
    [(a i -> a ; unAcc
               ; (s ys -> c (s ; Rng) 0 ; runS
                        ; (r y -> (r ; unRng) (y ys ; cons) ; Acc)))]
def sReport =
    c ->
    ((drawsWanted ; range) ; (c ; sInto) (theSeed nil ; Acc) ... ; fold)
      ; unAcc ; (s ys -> ys ; reverse ; Draws)

model Sampler in Prob(Samp, Draws) =
    compose   = sCompose
    embed     = sEmbed
    first     = sFirst
    observe   = sObserve
    sample    = sSample
    flip      = sFlip
    uniform   = sUniform
    condition = sCondition
    report    = sReport

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Model three: `Nondet` — the support, and nothing else
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The possibilistic Markov category: a weight is a bit, so the
# hom-object is a function into a plain list and `compose` is `flatMap`.
# It is here because it is the model that says what a weight ADDS, by
# taking them all away.  The seven doctrine laws run over it word for
# word, and the copy/two-flips distinction SURVIVES: `twoEqual`'s
# support is {HH, TT} and `twoIndep`'s is {HH, HT, TH, TT}, which is
# the possibilistic shadow of the same fact.  A model with no numbers
# in it still tells copying from sampling — what the numbers add is
# HOW MUCH, not WHETHER.

data Reach(b)   = List(b)
data Poss(a, b) = Fn⟨a =Recursive> Reach(b)⟩

def atP      = (c x -> c ; unPoss ; _ x ; ev ; unReach)
def nEmbed   = (f -> [(x -> f ; _ x ; ev ; single ; Reach)] ; Poss)
def nCompose = (c e -> [(x -> (c x ; atP) ; [(y -> e y ; atP)] ... ; map ; concat ; Reach)] ; Poss)
def nFirst =
    c ->
    [(p -> p ; unPair ; (x r -> (c x ; atP) ; [(y -> y r ; Pair)] ... ; map ; Reach))] ; Poss
def nFlip      = [(p -> true false ; pack ; Reach)] ; Poss
def nUniform   = [(n -> n ; range ; Reach)] ; Poss
def nCondition = [(p -> p ; unPair ; (b y -> b (y ; single) nil ; select ; Reach))] ; Poss
def nSample    = [(x -> (x ; _ 1 ; +) (x 2 ; *) ; pack ; Reach)] ; Poss
def nObserve   = (c -> ((c 3 ; atP) ; sum) ((c 4 ; atP) ; sum) ; +)
def nReport    = (c -> c ; unPoss ; _ 0 ; ev)

model Nondet in Prob(Poss, Reach) =
    compose   = nCompose
    embed     = nEmbed
    first     = nFirst
    observe   = nObserve
    sample    = nSample
    flip      = nFlip
    uniform   = nUniform
    condition = nCondition
    report    = nReport

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. The words a Markov program needs, and where they come from
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `first` acts on the DEEPER wire of a pairing.  Acting on the other one
# is `swap ; first ; swap`, and the swap is an ordinary base program
# embedded — which is the whole construction of the monoidal product
# `f ⊗ g` out of a strength, written once per model because a template
# `in Prob` is not instantiable by `with Enum` (§12, pragmatics).

def swapP = unPair ; swap ; Pair

def secondE in Enum =
    (c -> (([swapP] ; embed) (c ; first) ; compose) ; _ ([swapP] ; embed) ; compose)
def secondS in Sampler =
    (c -> (([swapP] ; embed) (c ; first) ; compose) ; _ ([swapP] ; embed) ; compose)
#   secondE : Kern(a, b) ⇒ Kern(Pair(c, a), Pair(c, b))

## two independent flips, as `flip ⊗ flip`: the strength twice, with a
## swap between.  This is the word the thesis turns on.
def bothFlipE in Enum =
    (flip ; first)
    _ ([swapP] ; embed) ; compose
    _ (flip ; first) ; compose
    _ ([swapP] ; embed) ; compose
def bothFlipS in Sampler =
    (flip ; first)
    _ ([swapP] ; embed) ; compose
    _ (flip ; first) ; compose
    _ ([swapP] ; embed) ; compose
#   bothFlipE : • ⇒ Kern(Pair(Float, Float), Pair(Bool, Bool)) — a word
#   of the category built BY HAND, so `in` and not `with`, and it
#   composes inside a `with Enum` scope like any transported stage.

## the biases, as ordinary base stages: one wire in, one wire out, so
## the functor embeds them and nothing knows they are parameters
def half      = (n -> 0.5)
def twoBiases = (n -> 0.5 0.5)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. COPY IS NOT NATURAL — the two programs, and the two distributions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

def twoEqualE with Enum = half ; flip ; dup
def twoIndepE with Enum = twoBiases ; bothFlipE
#   twoEqualE : Int =Enum Recursive> Pair(Bool, Bool)
#   twoIndepE : Int =Enum Recursive> Pair(Bool, Bool) — the SAME type,
#   and `with Enum` is the only reason either of them is a kernel at all.

def twoEqualS with Sampler = half ; flip ; dup
def twoIndepS with Sampler = twoBiases ; bothFlipS
def bothFlipN in Nondet =
    (flip ; first)
    _ ([swapP] ; embed) ; compose
    _ (flip ; first) ; compose
    _ ([swapP] ; embed) ; compose
def twoEqualN with Nondet = half ; flip ; dup
def twoIndepN with Nondet = twoBiases ; bothFlipN
#   `bothFlipE` is ENUM's word, and the elaborator says so if it is
#   written here: "bothFlipE is a word of Enum, so it builds a carrier
#   rather than being a program the embedding could embed".  A category
#   is entered from the base, and each model enters its own.

## reading a distribution as a FRAME — outcome, weight — with the
## weights summed per outcome by the frame library's `groupBy`
def showBits = (p -> p ; unPair ; (a b -> (a "H" "T" ; select) (b "H" "T" ; select) ; cat))
def tallyW =
    sh w ->
    w ; unWeighted
      ; (es -> ((es ; [(b -> b ; unBox ; (y p -> sh ; _ y ; ev))] ... ; map) \
                (es ; [(b -> b ; unBox ; (y p -> p))] ... ; map) ; groupBy) \
             ; [(g -> (g ; key) ((g ; part ; sumF) ; toStr) ; pack)] ... ; map)
def tallyD =
    sh d ->
    d ; unDraws
      ; (ys -> ((ys ; [(y -> sh ; _ y ; ev)] ... ; map) ys ; groupBy) \
             ; [(g -> (g ; key) ((g ; part ; len) ; toStr) ; pack)] ... ; map)
def distHead  = "outcome" "weight" ; pack
def countHead = "outcome" "draws" ; pack

"" ; print
"== one flip, copied — `with Enum = half ; flip ; dup` ==" ; print
distHead ([showBits] (twoEqualE ; eReport) ; tallyW) ; printFrame
#   HH | 0.5      the coin, and the same bit twice
#   TT | 0.5

"" ; print
"== two flips — `with Enum = twoBiases ; bothFlipE` ==" ; print
distHead ([showBits] (twoIndepE ; eReport) ; tallyW) ; printFrame
#   HH | 0.25     HT | 0.25     TH | 0.25     TT | 0.25
#   — 1/2 · 1/2 four times, and every weight is exact

"" ; print
"== the same two programs, SAMPLED (200 draws, seed 20260915) ==" ; print
countHead ([showBits] (twoEqualS ; sReport) ; tallyD) ; printFrame
#   TT | 98    HH | 102 — two cells, and the other two are ABSENT, not
#   small: a copied bit cannot disagree with itself at any seed
"" ; print
countHead ([showBits] (twoIndepS ; sReport) ; tallyD) ; printFrame
#   TH | 61   HT | 49   TT | 38   HH | 52 — four cells, 200 draws,
#   50 expected in each.  The seed is fixed, so these ten numbers are
#   the program's output and not a report about a run of it.

"" ; print
"== and possibilistically: the SUPPORT, weights forgotten ==" ; print
(twoEqualN ; nReport ; unReach) ; [showBits] ... ; map ; [(a b -> (a " " ; cat) b ; cat)] "" ... ; fold ; print
(twoIndepN ; nReport ; unReach) ; [showBits] ... ; map ; [(a b -> (a " " ; cat) b ; cat)] "" ... ; fold ; print

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. MONTY HALL, under `Enum`, with `condition`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The car is uniform on three doors, the guess is door 0, and the host
# opens a door that is neither.  When the car IS behind door 0 the host
# has a free choice and flips for it; otherwise the door is forced.
# That dependence is the whole problem, and writing it needs the coin
# to land beside the car — which is `second (flip)`, the strength with
# a swap around it.
#
# (Conditioning a UNIFORM host on "the host missed the car" is a
# different problem — Monty *Fall* — and it answers 1/2.  The model has
# to say what the host does; `condition` cannot repair a wrong one.)

def coinSndE in Enum = (([half] ; embed) flip ; compose) ; secondE
def coinSndS in Sampler = (([half] ; embed) flip ; compose) ; secondS
#   coinSndE : • ⇒ Kern(Pair(c, Int), Pair(c, Bool)) — flip a fair coin
#   ON TOP of whatever is riding below

def three    = (n -> 3)
def hostFrom =
    p -> p ; unPair
           ; (car c -> car ((car 0 ; equals) (c 1 2 ; select) (3 car ; -) ; select) ; Pair)

def montyE with Enum =
    three
    uniform
    dup
    coinSndE
    hostFrom
#   montyE : Int =Enum Recursive> Pair(Int, Int) — the JOINT of (car, host)

def showCH =
    p -> p ; unPair ; (car h -> (("car " (car ; toStr) ; cat) ", host " ; cat) (h ; toStr) ; cat)

"" ; print
"== Monty Hall: the joint distribution of (car, host) ==" ; print
distHead ([showCH] (montyE ; eReport) ; tallyW) ; printFrame
#   car 0, host 1 | 0.16666666666666666    1/3 · 1/2
#   car 0, host 2 | 0.16666666666666666    1/3 · 1/2
#   car 1, host 2 | 0.3333333333333333     1/3, the coin summed away
#   car 2, host 1 | 0.3333333333333333     1/3, likewise
#   — 1/3 is 0.3333333333333333 as a double and its half is exact,
#     so the two 1/6 rows really are the same number

## the evidence: the host opened door 1.  `condition` takes a Bool
## beside the value it is keeping, so the stage before it PAIRS the
## verdict with what survives.
def keepHost1 = (p -> p ; unPair ; (car h -> (h 1 ; equals) car ; Pair))

def montyPostE with Enum =
    three
    uniform
    dup
    coinSndE
    hostFrom
    keepHost1
    condition
#   montyPostE : Int =Enum Recursive> Int — the car, on the branches
#   where the host opened door 1, at their UNNORMALIZED weights

## Bayes' rule, as a frame operation: divide every weight by the mass.
def renorm =
    w ->
    w ; unWeighted
      ; (es -> (es ; [(b -> b ; unBox ; (y p -> p))] ... ; map ; sumF) \
             ; (m -> es ; [(b -> b ; unBox ; (y p -> y (p m ; fdiv) ; Box))] ... ; map \
                        ; Weighted))
def showCar = (n -> "car " (n ; toStr) ; cat)

"" ; print
"== …given the host opened door 1 — the posterior over the car ==" ; print
distHead ([showCar] (montyPostE ; eReport ; renorm) ; tallyW) ; printFrame
#   car 0 | 0.3333333333333333    (1/6) / (1/2) — staying wins 1/3
#   car 2 | 0.6666666666666666    (1/3) / (1/2) — SWITCHING WINS 2/3

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. A NOISY SENSOR, under `Enum` — the same three lines, different
#    numbers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prior: rain with probability 1/4.  The sensor cries rain with
# probability 7/8 when it rains and 1/8 when it does not.  Every number
# is dyadic, so every weight below is an exact double.

def quarter  = (n -> 0.25)
def biasOf2  = (r x -> r (r 0.875 0.125 ; select))   # Bool Bool ⇒ Bool Float
def flipSndE in Enum = flip ; secondE
def keepAlarm = (p -> p ; unPair ; (r a -> a r ; Pair))
def showRain = (b -> b "rain" "dry" ; select)

def sensorE with Enum =
    quarter
    flip
    dup
    biasOf2
    flipSndE
#   sensorE : Int =Enum Recursive> Pair(Bool, Bool) — (rain?, alarm?)

def showRA =
    p -> p ; unPair ; (r a -> ((r ; showRain) ", alarm " ; cat) (a "yes" "no" ; select) ; cat)

def sensorPostE with Enum =
    quarter
    flip
    dup
    biasOf2
    flipSndE
    keepAlarm
    condition

"" ; print
"== a noisy sensor: the joint of (rain, alarm) ==" ; print
distHead ([showRA] (sensorE ; eReport) ; tallyW) ; printFrame
#   rain, alarm yes | 0.21875     1/4 · 7/8
#   rain, alarm no  | 0.03125     1/4 · 1/8
#   dry,  alarm yes | 0.09375     3/4 · 1/8
#   dry,  alarm no  | 0.65625     3/4 · 7/8   — and they sum to 1.0

"" ; print
"== …given the alarm — the posterior over rain ==" ; print
distHead ([showRain] (sensorPostE ; eReport ; renorm) ; tallyW) ; printFrame
#   rain | 0.7      0.21875 / 0.3125 = 7/10, and 7/32 ÷ 5/16 is exact
#   dry  | 0.3      0.09375 / 0.3125 = 3/10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. A MARKOV CHAIN, under `Sampler`, with `with Recursive`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# A random walk on the integers: at each step flip a fair coin and move
# one either way.  `walkN` builds the n-step kernel by COMPOSING the
# one-step kernel n times — composition in the category, so the knot
# never sees a sample, only an arrow.

def idS in Sampler = [_] ; embed
def thenS in Sampler = compose
def moveBy = (p -> p ; unPair ; (x b -> b (x ; _ 1 ; +) (x 1 ; -) ; select))

def stepS with Sampler = dup ; coinSndS ; moveBy
#   stepS : Int =Sampler Recursive> Int

def walkN with Recursive =
    n -> (n 0 ; equals) [idS] [(stepS ((n ; _ 1 ; -) ; walkN) ; thenS)] ... ; cond
#   walkN : Int =Recursive> Samp(Int, Int) — a KERNEL built by
#   recursion, and the recursion is over the number of steps, not over
#   the randomness

"" ; print
"== a 10-step random walk, 200 draws (seed 20260915) ==" ; print
countHead ([toStr] ((10 ; walkN) ; sReport) ; tallyD) ; printFrame
#   -2 | 43   4 | 32   -4 | 19   2 | 33   0 | 52   10 | 1
#    6 |  9  -6 |  9    8 |  2
#   Ten steps from 0, so every endpoint is even and in [-10, 10], and
#   the exact distribution is binomial: 200·C(10,5)/1024 = 39.1 at 0,
#   200·C(10,4)/1024 = 41.0 at ±2, 20.5 at ±4, 8.2 at ±6, 2.0 at ±8,
#   0.2 at ±10.  The 200 draws sum: 43+32+19+33+52+1+9+9+2 = 200.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. THE MARKOV LAWS, stated as programs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two of them, and they pull in opposite directions.
#
# COPY IS NOT NATURAL.  This is the law that must FAIL, and the file
# shows it failing by running both sides.  In the BASE it holds and the
# normalizer PROVES it, for every word and every input; under the
# functor the two sides are different distributions, and `eq?` on the
# reports decides it false at every model.  That is the whole content
# of "a Markov category is not cartesian": the same diagram, two
# answers, and which answer you get is the functor.
#
# DISCARD IS NATURAL.  Every kernel has total mass 1 — `f ; discard =
# discard` — which is Fritz's causality/semicartesian axiom.  There is
# no `•` object to state it at, so it is stated at the mass, which is
# the same claim read through `report`.  `condition` is what breaks it,
# and the mass says so: a category with `condition` is AFFINE (mass ≤
# 1), not Markov, and Bayes' rule is exactly the renormalizing that
# puts the mass back.

## `says` comes from the frame library too — a Bool, spelled
def weightsOf = (w -> w ; unWeighted ; [(b -> b ; unBox ; (y p -> p))] ... ; map)
def massOf   = (c -> c ; eReport ; weightsOf ; sumF)
def sameDist = (u v -> (u ; eReport ; unWeighted) (v ; eReport ; unWeighted) ; eq? ; verdict)

"" ; print
"== copy is natural in the BASE — decided, for every word ==" ; print
[dup ; toStr toStr] [toStr ; dup] ; sameCode ; says ; print      # true — PROVED
[toStr ; dup] [dup ; toStr _] ; sameCode ; says ; print          # false — and it
                                                                 # really discriminates
"== …and NOT under the functor — decided at the distributions ==" ; print
twoEqualE twoIndepE ; sameDist ; says ; print                    # false

"" ; print
"== discard is natural: every kernel has mass 1 ==" ; print
twoEqualE ; massOf ; print                # 1.0
twoIndepE ; massOf ; print                # 1.0
montyE    ; massOf ; print                # 0.9999999999999999 — six
                                          #   branches of 1/6, and 1/6 is
                                          #   the double 1/3 halved; six of
                                          #   them summed left to right land
                                          #   one ulp under 1.  The claim is
                                          #   exact, the arithmetic is not,
                                          #   and saying which is which is
                                          #   the whole of stating a law at
                                          #   Float (cf. autodiff.braid §1)
sensorE   ; massOf ; print                # 1.0 — every weight dyadic
"== …until `condition`, which is what makes it AFFINE, not Markov ==" ; print
montyPostE  ; massOf ; print              # 0.5     — 1/6 + 1/3
sensorPostE ; massOf ; print              # 0.3125  — 7/32 + 3/32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. EXPECTATION IS A HOMOMORPHISM — but not of THIS category
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# `transformation Expect : Enum ⇒ <anything>` cannot be declared, and
# the reason is not a gap in the checker: E[g(X)] ≠ g(E[X]), so
# expectation does not preserve composition and is not a functor of the
# Markov category at all.  §12 prints the counterexample.
#
# What expectation IS a homomorphism of is the CONVEX structure — the
# fair-mixture operation — and that is an algebraic theory over one
# carrier, so it is declared here and machine-checked.  `mix` is the
# midpoint ½x + ½y; the laws are the barycentric ones (idempotence,
# commutativity, mediality).

theory Convex(m) =
    mix     : m m ⇒ m
    sample  : • ⇒ m
    other   : • ⇒ m
    observe : m ⇒ Float
    law mixIdem = (sample ; dup ; mix ; observe) (sample ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law mixComm = (sample other ; mix ; observe) (other sample ; mix ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict
    law medial  = ((sample other ; mix) (sample other ; mix) ; mix ; observe) ((sample sample ; mix) (other other ; mix) ; mix ; observe) ; eq? ; verdict

## the model that keeps the whole distribution: mixing halves both
## sides' weights and appends
def halveW  = (w -> w ; unWeighted ; [(e -> e ; unBox ; (y p -> y (p 0.5 ; fmul) ; Box))] ... ; map)
def mixW    = (u v -> (u ; halveW) (v ; halveW) ; append ; Weighted)
def meanW   =
    w ->
    w ; unWeighted ; [(acc e -> e ; unBox ; (y p -> acc (y p ; fmul) ; fadd))] 0.0 ... ; fold
def twoPts  = ((1.0 0.5 ; Box) (3.0 0.5 ; Box) ; pack) ; Weighted     # mean 2.0
def onePt   = (4.0 1.0 ; Box) ; single ; Weighted                     # mean 4.0

model Mixtures in Convex(Weighted(Float)) =
    mix     = mixW
    sample  = twoPts
    other   = onePt
    observe = meanW

## …and the model that keeps only the mean
def meanMix = (a b -> (a b ; fadd) 2.0 ; fdiv)

model Means in Convex(Float) =
    mix     = meanMix
    sample  = 2.0
    other   = 4.0
    observe = id

transformation Expect in Mixtures ⇒ Means = meanW
#   ONE component, because `Convex` has one parameter.  The four
#   squares say: the mean of a fair mixture is the mean of the means
#   (`mix`), the evidence corresponds (`sample`, `other`), and the
#   model's own observation IS the component (`observe`).  `:transformations`
#   prints the verdicts.

"" ; print
"== expectation, as a homomorphism of the convex structure ==" ; print
(twoPts onePt ; mixW ; Expect) ; print         # 3.0  — ½·2 + ½·4
(twoPts ; Expect) (onePt ; Expect) ; meanMix ; print
                                               # 3.0  — the same, the other way
                                               #   round the square

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. WHAT IS REFUSED, and what that establishes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXPECTATION IS NOT A FUNCTOR.  A functor preserves composition, and
# expectation does not: square the uniform on {0, 1, 2} and the mean
# moves from 1 to 5/3, while squaring the mean leaves 1.

def sqr    = (x -> x x ; *)
def uni3 with Enum = three ; uniform
def uni3sq with Enum = three ; uniform ; sqr

"" ; print
"== E[X] and E[X^2] for X uniform on {0, 1, 2} ==" ; print
uni3   ; eReport ; eExpect ; print      # 1.0                 = (0+1+2)/3
uni3sq ; eReport ; eExpect ; print      # 1.6666666666666665  = (0+1+4)/3
                                        #   5/3 is not a double; this is the
                                        #   nearest one, and (1+4)·(1/3)
                                        #   summed left to right is how it
                                        #   got there.  1.0 squared is 1.0,
                                        #   so E[X^2] ≠ (E[X])^2 and `Expect`
                                        #   is not a functor of `Prob`.
#
# `transformation Draw : Sampler ⇒ Enum` HAS NO COMPONENT, and the
# reason is mathematical rather than mechanical: a component is a word
# `Samp(a, b) ⇒ Kern(a, b)` that is generic in `a` and `b`, so it can
# never APPLY the sampler — and a sample is not a function of the
# distribution anyway.  Writing the obvious wrong one (the point mass
# at the draw taken from a fixed seed) type-checks and is then refused
# at the samples, because a fresh seed per factor is not the same
# kernel as one seed threaded through the composite:
#
#     transformation Draw : Sampler ⇒ Enum = asDirac, asDiracD
#     error: transformation Draw: the square for slot 'compose' does not
#     commute at the theory's samples — a transformation between models
#     is a homomorphism, slot by slot.
#
# `transformation Support : Enum ⇒ Nondet` is the one that OUGHT to
# work — forgetting the weights really is a homomorphism, it is the
# support functor from the distribution monad to the nonempty powerset
# — and it is refused for a reason worth writing down.  Every square
# but one is fine; the `first` square is not:
#
#     error: transformation Support: the square for slot 'first' does not
#     commute at the theory's samples — … The two carriers were compared
#     with `eq?` directly, because the theory declares no exit whose
#     input FITS this slot's result — an exit at `k(Int, Int)` does not
#     reach the hom-object at a PAIRING, which is what `first` leaves.
#
# (That message named the wrong thing until 2026-09-15: it said the
# theory declares NO exit, and this theory declares two.  A refusal
# that misreports its own cause sends the reader to the wrong repair,
# so it now says `no exit whose input FITS`.)
#
# A second exit at the pairing cannot help either, and this is the
# sharp end of it: the square THAT exit would itself need is fed by the
# theory's first entry — `sample : • ⇒ k(Int, Int)`, the doctrine's —
# whose object is `Int` and not a pair, so the exit's own square does
# not even typecheck.  The carriers are therefore compared directly,
# and they are closures.
#
# The general statement, which is the pragmatics item this file buys:
# a component must be generic in the hom-object's arguments, so it can
# never RUN the carrier; a square between two function-carrier models is
# therefore decidable only when the normalizer proves it, which needs
# the component to pass the witness through untouched.
# `examples/transformations.braid`'s `Forget : Names ⇒ Funcs` is exactly
# that case (drop a `Str` field) and proves all five.  A component that
# TRANSFORMS the witness — which is what forgetting a weight is — is out
# of reach at the pairing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 13. What is NOT built, plainly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTINUOUS DISTRIBUTIONS.  Everything here is finitely supported.
# `Enum` enumerates and `Nondet` lists; a Gaussian has no list.  The
# carrier would be a density against a base measure, which is the
# `Density` model this file does not ship — and not because it is
# tedious: scoring a density needs the OUTCOME as an input, so its
# `flip` would be `k(Pair(Float, Bool), Bool)` and not `k(Float, Bool)`.
# A slot has ONE type for every model, so a scoring semantics is a model
# of a DIFFERENT theory, and saying that is more use than shipping a
# third carrier that repeats `Sampler`'s threading with `Weight` in
# place of `Rng`.
#
# INFERENCE.  There is exact enumeration and there is forward sampling.
# There is no importance sampling, no MCMC, no variable elimination.
# `condition` is rejection at weight 0/1 and `renorm` is Bayes' rule on
# the frame; anything cleverer is an algorithm, and this file is about
# what the CATEGORY is.
#
# DISINTEGRATION.  Cho & Jacobs' Bayesian inversion (READING) takes a
# kernel `k(a, b)` and a distribution on `a` and returns a kernel
# `k(b, a)` — the posterior as an arrow.  In `Enum` it is writable:
# enumerate the joint, group by the `b` coordinate, renormalize inside
# each group, and rebuild a kernel by lookup.  What stops it being a
# screenful is the lookup: `k(b, a)` must answer for EVERY `b`, and a
# table keyed by an arbitrary outcome type needs an ordering or a hash,
# which the language does not have (`eq?` is structural, `lt?` is Int).
# §7 and §8 are disintegration at one point, done with `condition` and
# `renorm` instead — which is the operation, without the arrow.
#
# NO CHOICE SLOT.  A row program that branches into two different
# KERNELS cannot be written inside a `with` scope: transporting a sum
# needs ArrowChoice' `left`, and the Doctrine has `first` and not
# `left`.  `hostFrom` and `biasOf2` sidestep it the way
# `examples/frame.braid`'s `keep` does — the deciding is deterministic
# and rides the wire, and the only randomness is a generator composed
# unconditionally.  `coinSndE` flips even when the coin will be ignored,
# which is why §7's tally has to SUM the two branches that agree.
